1. What This App Is Made Of :

A standard MERN stack application consists of three main pieces:
Frontend (React.js): The user interface and admin dashboard where users browse products and make purchases.

Backend (Node.js & Express.js): The API server that processes payments, handles product queries, and manages business logic.

Database (MongoDB): The database storing users, orders, and product details.  

Direct EC2 Deployment (Simplest Path)
You deploy the application directly onto a single AWS EC2 virtual machine.

    How it works: You use Terraform to spin up an EC2 instance, install Node.js/Nginx via shell scripts, clone the GitHub repository onto the server, and connect it to MongoDB Atlas (cloud database).

    Best for: Learning fundamental server setup, SSH connections, and basic cloud automation.

Dockerized Container Deployment (Recommended Next Step)
You containerize the frontend and backend using Docker, then run them anywhere seamlessly.

    How it works: You write a Dockerfile for the Node backend and React frontend, and combine them using docker-compose. You then run these containers on an EC2 instance or AWS ECS.

    Best for: Ensuring the app runs identically on your local machine and in the cloud, removing environment configuration issues.

EKS Kubernetes Cluster (Advanced Production Path)
You deploy the containerized app into an AWS EKS Kubernetes cluster for high availability and auto-scaling.

    How it works: You build the Docker images, upload them to Amazon ECR, write Kubernetes deployment.yaml and service.yaml manifests, and deploy them using kubectl to worker nodes created via Terraform.

    Best for: Simulating real-world production environments where services need automatic failover and load balancing.

STEPS N PROCESSES
Step 1: Cloning the Repository
The very first action in local development is downloading the project's source code from GitHub onto your local computer.

Step 2: Inspecting the Project Structure & Installing Backend Dependencies
MERN projects typically separate the server code (Backend) from the user interface (Frontend). We need to install the software libraries (packages) required by the backend first.

Move into the backend folder (or inspect if package.json is in the root directory) and install backend packages:
npm install

Step 3: Setting Up Environment Variables (.env)
Backend applications need sensitive information—like database passwords, secret keys, and port numbers—to function properly. Instead of hardcoding these inside the code, Node.js reads them from a hidden file named .env

Step 3 (Continued): Setting up the Backend .env
For a MERN project, the .env file belongs in the Backend root folder because that is where the Express server connects to MongoDB and signs secret tokens.
cd Backend,cp .env.example .env or touch .env 
What should go inside the Backend .env file?
Inside your backend .env, paste these standard MERN variables:
PORT=5000
MONGO_URI=mongodb://127.0.0.1:27017/ecommerce
JWT_SECRET=mysecretkey12345
1. Purpose
    PORT: Tells the backend Node/Express server which network port to run on (Port 5000 is standard).

    MONGO_URI: Tells the app where your MongoDB database lives.

    JWT_SECRET: A secret string used to encrypt user login tokens.

1. How to get the MONGO_URI You have two choices for your database:
Choice A: Local MongoDB (Free & Offline)
If you have MongoDB installed on your computer, the default connection URL is:mongodb://127.0.0.1:27017/ecommerce
127.0.0.1:27017 is your local computer's database port.
/ecommerce is the name MongoDB will automatically give to your project's database.

Choice B: MongoDB Atlas (Free Cloud Database - Recommended if you don't have local MongoDB)Go to mongodb.com/cloud/atlas and create a free account.Create a free M0 Cluster.
Create a database username and password under Database Access.Under Network Access, add 0.0.0.0/0 (allow access from anywhere).Click Connect $\rightarrow$ Drivers to copy your connection string. It will look like this:mongodb+sandbox://username:password@cluster0.abcde.mongodb.net/ecommerce

Installing MongoDB on Ubuntu:
#!/bin/bash

# Exit immediately if a command fails
set -e

echo "=== Installing Prerequisites ==="
sudo apt-get install gnupg curl -y

echo "=== Importing MongoDB GPG Key ==="
curl -fsSL https://www.mongodb.org/static/pgp/server-7.0.asc | \
   sudo gpg -o /usr/share/keyrings/mongodb-server-7.0.gpg --dearmor

echo "=== Adding MongoDB Repository ==="
echo "deb [ arch=amd64,arm64 signed-by=/usr/share/keyrings/mongodb-server-7.0.gpg ] https://repo.mongodb.org/apt/ubuntu jammy/mongodb-org/7.0 multiverse" | sudo tee /etc/apt/sources.list.d/mongodb-org-7.0.list

echo "=== Updating Package List & Installing MongoDB ==="
sudo apt-get update
sudo apt-get install -y mongodb-org

echo "=== Starting & Enabling MongoDB Service ==="
sudo systemctl start mongod
sudo systemctl enable mongod

echo "=== Checking Service Status ==="
sudo systemctl status mongod

chmod +x install_mongo.sh && ./install_mongo.sh

Step 2: Install MongoDB directly
Now run the package manager update and install MongoDB directly:
sudo apt-get update && sudo apt-get install -y mongodb-org

Step 3: Start the MongoDB Service
Once installation completes, enable and start the service:
